  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Johny vs Surabhi Movies on 30 June, 2018",
    "facts": "The first respondent herein filed (O.P) RC No. 3 of 2015 before the Rent Control Court, Chalakudy seeking an order of eviction under Sections 11(3), 11(8) and 11(4)(i) of the Act. The parties are referred to as in the Original Petition. According to petitioner, he bona fide needs the petition schedule building for starting a digital press, including wedding photography. Now he is doing his digital press in a small shop room which forms part of the building in which the petition schedule shop room is situated. Thus, an additional accommodation is required for the expansion of his Johny vs Surabhi Movies on 30 June, 2018 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/17899544/ 2 existing business. The respondents are not mainly depending on the income from the business carried on in the petition schedule building and several other vacant rooms are available in the locality to shift their business. The petitioner has no other buildings of his own for the additional accommodation. 3. The respondents resisted the said petition contending that the bona fide need projected in the petition is a ruse for eviction only. According to respondents 1 and 2, they have not sublet the the petition schedule building to the third respondent and they are mainly depending upon the income from the business carried on in the petition schedule shop room. They denied the allegation that they have sublet the petition schedule building to the third respondent. Further, according to them, the petition schedule shop room is not actually required for the expansion of the said business. No other buildings are available in the locality to shift their business from the tenanted premises. The third respondent is not the Proprietor of the business concern and he is only an employee of the first respondent and the rent deed does not prohibit subletting. 4. On the rival pleas, both parties adduced evidence and after considering the evidence on record, the Rent Control Court passed an order of eviction under Sections 11(3), 11(4)(i) and 11(8) of the Act on the findings that the need projected in the petition is bona fide, that respondents 1 and 2 are not entitled to get protection under the second proviso to Section 11(3) of the Act, and that respondents 1 and 2 have sublet the petition schedule building to the third respondent and that the petition schedule building is required for additional accommodation , thereby the petitioner is entitled to get vacant possession of the shop room under Sections 11(4)(i), 11(3) and 11(8) of the Act. Eventhough the respondents had preferred an appeal as RCA No. 171 of 2016 before the appellate authority, after re-appreciating the evidence on record, the appellate authority also concurred with the findings of the Rent Control Court and dismissed the appeal. Thus the concurrent findings whereby the courts below have granted an order of eviction under Sections 11(3), 11(8) and 11(4)(i) of he Act are challenged in this revision.",
    "arguments": "Though this revision petition has been filed on various grounds, the learned counsel for the revision petitioner mainly contended that the courts below have failed to appreciate the evidence on record under Section 11(4)(i) and 11(3) of the Act in its correct perspective. According to the learned counsel for the petitioner, there is absolutely no evidence to prove that the first respondent has sublet the petition schedule building to the third respondent and there is bona fides in the need. Therefore, the sole question that arises for consideration in this revision is whether there is any illegality or impropriety in the concurrent findings of the courts below that the petitioner has succeeded in proving his case that he bona fide needs the tenanted premises and the first respondent had sublet the petition schedule building to the third respondent.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "Though this revision petition has been filed on various grounds, the learned counsel for the revision petitioner mainly contended that the courts below have failed to appreciate the evidence on record under Section 11(4)(i) and 11(3) of the Act in its correct perspective.",
    "respondent_arguments": "According to the learned counsel for the petitioner, there is absolutely no evidence to prove that the first respondent has sublet the petition schedule building to the third respondent and there is bona fides in the need. Therefore, the sole question that arises for consideration in this revision is whether there is any illegality or impropriety in the concurrent findings of the courts below that the petitioner has succeeded in proving his case that he bona fide needs the tenanted premises and the first respondent had sublet the petition schedule building to the third respondent.",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
